※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none host=host.c
※ pl4g-test: args -shared

※ `@[host("SYMBOL")]` on a function with no body says the program this one is
※ loaded into defines it, under that symbol.  It is looked up with `dlsym` the
※ first time it is called, kept, and called from then on with the system's
※ convention.
※
※ **What crosses is what C has**: integers, floating-point numbers, truth
※ values, characters and references, a reference handed over as the address of
※ the place it names -- an `@[abi]` record among them -- and a `str` as C's text,
※ a nul-terminated copy made for the call and given back after it.
※
※ **This is a shared object** (`-shared`), which is what has a host: `host.c`
※ loads it, calls the one function it offers and checks what came back, and
※ that it ran the constructor when loaded and the destructor when unloaded.
※ Every argument register is among what it checks: the stub that finds each
※ function keeps them all while it asks.
※
※ Compare: **LuaJIT**'s `ffi.cdef`, which declares C functions and finds them by
※ name when first called; **Python**'s ctypes, whose `c_char_p` makes the same
※ copy of a string; **C**, **Zig** and **Rust**, whose `extern` declarations are
※ this one with a linker resolving them before the program starts.

@[abi]
type Pair =
    first : i64 ; second : f64

@[host("note")]
fn note(what: i64)

@[host("join_words")]
fn join_words(a: str, b: str) → i64

@[host("many")]
fn many(a: i64, b: i64, c: i64, d: i64, e: i64, f: i64, g: f64, h: f64) → i64

@[host("scale")]
fn scale(p: &mut Pair, by: f64) → bool

@[host("bump")]
fn bump(n: &mut i64)

@[host("next_char")]
fn next_char(c: char) → char

let started: mut i64 = 0i64

@[constructor, impure]
fn begin():
    started ← 1i64
    note(1i64)

@[destructor, impure]
fn finish():
    note(4i64)

※ What the host calls.  It follows the system's convention, as everything a
※ shared object offers has to (5303).
@[visible, cdecl, impure]
fn run(n: i64) → i64:
    let p: mut Pair = Pair(.first ← n, .second ← 1.5f64)
    let k: mut i64 = 40i64
    bump(&mut k)
    let words: i64 = join_words("host", "functions")
    let all: i64 = many(1i64, 2i64, 3i64, 4i64, 5i64, 6i64, 7f64, 0.5f64)
    let scaled: bool = scale(&mut p, 2f64)
    let c: char = next_char('a')
    if scaled ∧ c = 'b' ∧ p.first = 2i64 × n: k + words + all + started else: 0i64
